EXT. PUBLIC POOL - DIVING PLATFORM - LATE AFTERNOON

An empty municipal pool at closing time. The water is green-blue and still. A ten-meter platform rises above it, rust at the joints, a faded sign reading DIVE AT OWN RISK.

NOOR, fifteen, in a black one-piece and a wet towel around her shoulders, stands at the base of the ladder with her toes curled around the bottom rung, and FELIX, sixteen, her older cousin, in cutoffs and a tank top, sits in a plastic lifeguard chair eating sunflower seeds and spitting the shells into the pool, and neither of them says anything for a while, and the filter hums, and the drain gurgles, and a plane crosses the pink sky.

FELIX
You don't have to.

NOOR
I know.

FELIX
It's just a platform.

NOOR
It's ten meters.

FELIX
It's ten meters of nothing, then water.

NOOR
That's what I'm afraid of.

She puts a foot on the second rung. Takes it off. Felix watches without comment, rolling a seed between his teeth.

FELIX
Dad did it when he was fourteen.

NOOR
Uncle Rob never did anything he said he did.

FELIX
Okay, that's fair.

She climbs three rungs, then four, then stops, her knuckles pale on the rail. Felix stands up, tosses the empty seed bag into a trash can, and walks to the edge of the pool. He kneels there, looking up.

FELIX (CONT'D)
I'll be right here.

NOOR
You can't catch me from ten meters.

FELIX
I'll catch you after.

She exhales. Climbs. The rungs ring under her feet. At the top, she stands at the edge of the board with her arms wrapped around herself, her wet hair dripping. Far below, Felix is very small.

NOOR (CONT'D)
(calling down)
Don't watch.

FELIX
I'm watching.

NOOR
Felix!

FELIX
Okay, I'm not watching.

He covers his eyes with both hands. She laughs, a breath of a sound, and steps off.
